        <h3 style="font-size: 16pt; margin-bottom: 20px;">Admissions and Course Registration</h3>
        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">Admission Policy</h4>
            <p>Hebrew College welcomes students of all backgrounds and beliefs to join in the academic study of Judaism, and to become knowledgeable, creative participants, educators and leaders in the Jewish community and the larger world.</p>
            <p>Hebrew College offers graduate and undergraduate degrees and certificate programs in Jewish education and Jewish studies, as well as rabbinic ordination and cantorial ordination within a pluralistic setting. For detailed information about applying to any of our programs, please contact the Office of Admissions at {{ admissions_phone }} or admissions@hebrewcollege.edu.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">Statement of Nondiscrimination</h4>
            <p>Hebrew College does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, national or ethnic origin, gender, sexual orientation, age or disability in admission to, access to, treatment in, or employment in its programs and activities. Any student who is unable to participate in a program due to a disability may be eligible for accommodations. Students who are unable to participate in a program due to a disability must contact the Office of Student Services to request accommodations.</p>
            <p>Hebrew College is committed to providing an inclusive environment for all students. We strictly prohibit discrimination and harassment based on any protected characteristic. Any instances of discrimination or harassment should be reported immediately to the Office of the Dean of Students.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">Acceptance Deposit</h4>
            <p>Once you have been accepted to one of Hebrew College's academic programs, a $100 nonrefundable commitment deposit is required. This deposit will be applied to your first semester's tuition and fee charges. If you choose not to enroll in the program for which you were accepted, the deposit will not be refunded.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">Email Addresses for Students</h4>
            <p>Once you have been accepted to one of Hebrew College's academic programs, you will be required to complete an IT agreement and will be assigned a Hebrew College email address. All official emails from the college will be sent to this email. The Hebrew College IT department is responsible for maintaining the security and integrity of the college's email system. Students are responsible for the security of their email account and must keep their passwords confidential.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">Registration</h4>
            <p>You must register each semester to attend classes. Anyone attending a class who has not registered and who does not appear on the official class roster may be asked to leave the classroom until he or she has officially registered. You will only receive grades or credit for classes for which you are officially registered. All students—matriculated, nonmatriculated, for-credit and non-credit—are required to register for classes each semester. Registration is managed through the online MyHC Portal. Students must register for classes by the published deadlines. Failure to register for classes by the published deadlines will result in the student not being enrolled in the class and will not receive credit for the course.</p>
            <p>Payment of all tuition and fees is due at the time of registration. Hebrew College does not provide third-party billing. If tuition is being paid by an employer, payment should be made directly to the college. Alternatively, you can make the payment and arrange for payment to be made by your employer. For payment arrangements for student loans, please contact the Financial Aid Office. Students who fail to follow this procedure will not be considered registered and are not entitled to the student and academic services of the College.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">Cross-Registration Opportunities</h4>
            <p>Hebrew College participates in cross-registration agreements with the following colleges and universities, as well as other area accredited colleges and universities on a case-by-case basis:</p>
            <ul class="toc-list">
                <li>{{ partner_college_1 }}</li>
                <li>{{ partner_college_2 }}</li>
                <li>{{ partner_college_3 }}</li>
                <li>{{ partner_college_4 }}</li>
                <li>{{ partner_college_5 }}</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="page">
        <h3 style="font-size: 16pt; margin-bottom: 20px;">Dropping Courses, Withdrawals and Leaves</h3>
        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">Add/Drop Period</h4>
            <p>Hebrew College provides an Add/Drop Period during the beginning of each semester. You may add, drop or change from credit to noncredit or noncredit to credit during this period by completing an official "Add/Drop" form and submitting it to the Registrar's Office. The Add/Drop Period is subject to change and is published in the Academic Calendar. Students must complete the official form and submit it to the Registrar's Office by the published deadline. Failure to do so will result in the student being charged for the course.</p>
            <p>The Add/Drop Period lasts until the end of the first week of school. After the Add/Drop Period has ended, no additional class may be added or dropped, and the dropping of courses will be considered a withdrawal. A grade of "W" will appear on your transcript.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">Withdrawal from a Course</h4>
            <p>If you withdraw from a course after the Add/Drop Period, you must provide the official withdrawal form as notification to the Registrar's Office. The withdrawal form must be submitted by the published deadline for the course. If you withdraw from a course, you will receive a "W" grade on your transcript. Students who withdraw from a course after the published deadline will be charged for the course.</p>
            <p>If you withdraw from a course, you must receive a "W" grade. A formal withdrawal from class does not negate any financial obligation, except as stated in the Tuition Refund Policy. Students who withdraw from a course must also notify the Financial Aid Office.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">Students Receiving Financial Aid</h4>
            <p>Withdrawing from a course may have financial implications for those students receiving financial aid. Students who withdraw from a course must notify the Financial Aid Office immediately to determine the impact on their financial aid eligibility and funding.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">Failure to Withdraw from a Course</h4>
            <p>Students who discontinue studies without receiving leave of absence approval for a period of time may be granted a leave of absence for a specified period (not to exceed two semesters) with the written approval of the director of their academic program. You must complete the "Petition for Leave of Absence" form and submit it to the Office of the Registrar. A $100 registration fee must be paid for each semester of leave.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">Leave of Absence</h4>
            <p>A leave of absence may be requested in the following circumstances:</p>
            <ul class="toc-list">
                <li>You are a matriculated student who requires time off for personal, professional or other legitimate reasons before all coursework has been completed. You may take a leave for no longer than one year during your degree program.</li>
                <li>You are a matriculated student who is writing a thesis, but not attending classes for a specific semester.</li>
                <li>You are a matriculated student who has completed all coursework, including a thesis or final project, for a degree and are awaiting the next commencement. Registration fees will be waived.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Students who discontinue studies without receiving leave of absence approval will be made inactive and may be required to reapply for admission to the College when they wish to resume studies. They will be subject to all requirements that apply to newly admitted students.</p>
            <p>Students who are granted a leave of absence will be required to pay tuition and fees for the semester of leave, unless otherwise specified in writing by the Registrar's Office.</p>
            <p>Students holding student loans should consult with the Office of Student Financial Aid before taking a leave of absence.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="page">
        <h3 style="font-size: 16pt; margin-bottom: 20px;">Grades and Educational Records</h3>
        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">Grading System</h4>
            <p>Hebrew College grade-point averages and academic standards are based on the following grade-point equivalents: A+ = 4.3, A = 4.0, A- = 3.7, B+ = 3.3, B = 3.0, B- = 2.7, C+ = 2.3, C = 2.0, C- = 1.7, D+ = 1.3, D = 1.0, F, I, NC, P, W, PI, NG = 0</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">Incompletes</h4>
            <p>A student who, for compelling reasons postpones the submission of required coursework, must fill out a "Petition for Incomplete" form and submit it to the instructor and to the Office of the Registrar. Coursework must be completed and submitted by the student during the following semester.</p>
            <ul class="toc-list">
                <li>If an Incomplete is requested for a fall semester course, the outstanding work must be submitted by the last day of the following spring semester.</li>
                <li>If an Incomplete is requested for a spring semester course, the outstanding work must be submitted by the first day of the following fall semester.</li>
                <li>If an Incomplete is requested for a summer course, the outstanding work must be submitted by the last day of the fall semester.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Students who are granted an Incomplete must complete the outstanding work by the deadline specified by the instructor. Graduating students may not have any outstanding Incompletes on their transcripts at the beginning of their final semester.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">Extension</h4>
            <p>In special circumstances, the Provost has the authority to grant an extension for the submission of overdue work with the approval of the instructor. To request an extension, you must submit a written petition to the Registrar's Office, explaining the circumstances. The petition must include a due date by which work will be submitted. The Registrar will submit the petition to the Dean of your program for approval. Generally, the extension will be limited to two to four weeks.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">Standard Requirements for Degree Candidates</h4>
            <p><strong>Undergraduate Students</strong></p>
            <p>Students must maintain a grade-point average of 2.5 or above. Full-time undergraduate students must complete 12 credits per semester and finish their degree within five years of initial enrollment. Part-time students are those taking fewer than 12 credits per semester and must complete their degree within ten years.</p>
            <p><strong>Graduate Students</strong></p>
            <p>Students must maintain a minimum grade-point average of 3.0. Students in graduate programs only receive credit for courses in which they earn a grade of B or better. Full-time graduate students must complete 9 credits per semester and finish their degree within three years. Part-time students are those taking fewer than 9 credits per semester and must complete their degree within seven years. Students in the Rabbinical School are expected to complete their degree within four years. Students in the Shoolman Graduate School are expected to complete their degree within six years.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">Students Receiving Student Loans</h4>
            <p>Both undergraduate and graduate students who ask for student loans are required by the federal government to take a financial aid counseling session. Students must also complete the required financial aid paperwork. Students who receive student loans must maintain satisfactory academic progress each semester. Failure to do so can impact eligibility for further student loans. See the "Financial Aid at Hebrew College" section for more information.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">Extensions and Probation for Degree Candidates</h4>
            <p>Matriculated students requesting less than half-time study or extensions of time limits should consult with their advisors. Students who are placed on academic probation must meet with their advisor to develop a plan to meet the academic standards. Students on probation may be subject to additional requirements, including reduced course loads or mandatory tutoring. Students who are placed on academic probation may be subject to suspension or dismissal if they fail to meet the requirements of probation within the specified time frame.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">Accessing Educational Records</h4>
            <p><strong>Requests for Transcripts</strong></p>
            <p>For accessing your grades, you should contact the Office of the Registrar by completing the official transcript request form or by contacting the Registrar's Office directly. You may request transcripts for yourself or for another college, employer, institution or agency only at your written request. Requests must include the same name you used when attending Hebrew College and your social security number. Transcripts are available for a fee. Credit card information or a check or money order in U.S. dollars payable to Hebrew College must be included with the request. No transcripts will be issued if you have an unpaid balance in your student account. The Transcript Request form is available for download from the Registrar's page on the Hebrew College website.</p>
            <p><strong>Requests for Student Educational Records</strong></p>
            <p>"Student Educational Records" are all the documents relating to a student that are maintained by an educational institution. To request these records, you must submit a written request to the Office of the Registrar, identifying the records to be inspected. Written requests will receive a response within a reasonable time, but not more than 45 days after submission. The Registrar will arrange for access and will notify you (or your parent if you are a dependent student) and arrange for the release of the records. Further questions about the records and the process of requesting them should be directed to the Office of the Registrar.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">The Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act</h4>
            <p>Hebrew College does not release Student Educational Records to third parties, except by the respective student's written request or as authorized by law. Federal legislation, specifically the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA), ensures the confidentiality of these records.</p>
            <p>For complete information regarding FERPA legislation, read the most current FERPA Guide published by the American Association of Collegiate Registrars and Admissions Officers. In general, you have the right to:</p>
            <ul class="toc-list">
                <li>Inspect your Educational Records;</li>
                <li>Request that the College obtain your prior written consent before releasing personally identifiable information;</li>
                <li>Request that the College amend your Educational Records, if you believe the records are inaccurate or misleading.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Both Hebrew College policy and FERPA provide you with certain protections concerning the confidentiality of your Educational Records. No information about you will be released to any third party without your written consent unless otherwise authorized or required by law. The College, however, maintains the legal right to withhold the release of Directory Information to third parties who may not have an educational interest in the information.</p>
            <p>The College defines the following student information as Directory Information: name, date of birth, dates of enrollment, full or part-time status, degree or certificate program, area of specialization, Hebrew College ID image for degree and certificate candidates, and a campus mailing address. The College may release Directory Information without your written consent unless you have requested that the College withhold the release of Directory Information to third parties who may not have an educational interest in the information.</p>
            <p>To restrict the release or publication of any information, you must submit a written request to the Registrar's Office. No student information is released to third parties without the student's written consent, except as required by law. The College reserves the right to refuse to release any information if it determines that the release of the information would be detrimental to the student's welfare or the welfare of the College. The College reserves the right to refuse to release any information if it determines that the release of the information would be detrimental to the student's welfare or the welfare of the College.</p>
            <p>Under FERPA, Hebrew College is allowed to disclose, without your prior written consent, student information that is not considered Directory Information, to authorized officials of the College who have a legitimate educational interest in the information. An "authorized official" is defined as a person or entity with a legitimate educational interest in the information. The College determines whether a person or entity has a legitimate educational interest in the information. The College reserves the right to determine who has a legitimate educational interest in the information.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-section">
            <h4 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 5px;">Restricted Information</h4>
            <p>Under FERPA, students do not have a right to inspect information held by the College that is an Educational Record. These include:</p>
            <ul class="toc-list">
                <li>Medical records;</li>
                <li>Law enforcement records;</li>
                <li>Employment-related records;</li>
                <li>Records containing information about the individual after he or she is no longer a student.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>However, you may have rights to inspect such records under other laws. In addition, you do not have the right to access certain Educational Records, such as:</p>
            <ul class="toc-list">
                <li>Confidential letters of recommendation, if you have waived your right of access in writing.</li>
                <li>Financial records of your parents.</li>
                <li>Records of disciplinary actions taken against a student and personnel.</li>
                <li>Administrative records for a student who does not officially attend the program of admission. If you completed a course at Hebrew College, but never officially attended the program of admission, then you have no rights with respect to that course, but do not have rights with respect to the admissions credentials for that program.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

